d um all of the resources that they  put through for example like connecting  you to lawyers or accountants and  things like that they often will make a  point to do  things for specific industries in  specific regions so they would do things  for  social innovation for example  specifically but then also for  international  um things as well so they do have some a  lot of content that they cater  specifically for  um for that  has anybody seen like maybe classmates  trying to launch things internationally  or  you heard anything from on from that  front  yeah there's um there's a company from  my class called ajib  uh which is a robo advisor in indonesia  um i think overall like it's a process  not the idea and that also applies to  whether it's u.s or international  at the end of the day you're trying to  solve a problem or you know a pain point  as it's called  um and that exists everywhere in many  different forms  um so it's more about the process as  opposed to you know a specific idea  okay and so kind of on that similar  theme of  process versus idea can you are folks  able to apply the process to an idea  they had before the gsb  in some of these classes or are these  ideas that had to have come up  at the gsb  it can definitely be something you have  before gsb or came up with with  gsb i had my idea a similar version  before  actually when i was applying to gsb i  remember writing about in my application  and  then when i got to gsb i just started  exploring it through coursework  other people set up weekly chats with  friends to just brainstorm a ton of  ideas and then decide which one they  want to explore  while they're there  um my friend my friend chad monroe from  my class  runs startup called jupiter with two of  our classmates now  uh when he first got to campus i think  the original format of this idea was  shipping things from uh the us  outside via to bring goods that like  weren't otherwise accessible  um outside the us and now it's a  last-mile grocery delivery company and  that evolution kind of took place while  we were at the gsp  um in in rue he got you know two other  gsb co-founders and an engineer  um from an undergrad i think um so  anyway  all the all that is to say like you can  definitely start  you know early bring bring people along  look for people to join you on your ride  your idea will probably change  and you know international or us i think  that's all the world is your oyster  question about funding so getting  resources  and funding is going to be a big part of  the entrepreneurial journey  um there's a question from joshua about  resources to help you pitch to venture  capitalists for seed funding  um any experiences on that front  i i mean i would argue that this the  winter quarter  startup garage is designed to make you  help you  design your pitch deck like that's  that's the  the unsaid kind of you know intention  and  at the end of that um that class you do  actually get to pitch  live to to a couple of venture  capitalists that are invited by the  teaching team  and when i say a couple i don't mean  like two or three i mean there was like  10 of them there  right so like and they're all very  related to your um  you know your the area that you're  looking to raise from and and  from that class or how there have been a  lot of people who have gone forward and  raised capital  the same experience i had the same exact  experience in the um  in hacking for defense uh at the end  bunch of people pitched  some people actually got funded right so  um that the resources are there  and same thing with stanford venture  studios throughout the year they'll put  put out a lot of  um resources that you can go and look at  yourself to go look at  you know examples and walk-throughs and  things like that but you can also sit  down  with um a vc or an entrepreneur and go  through your deck  we did that you know on multiple  occasions and and  got a ton of good feedback from uh from  individuals  and shawan you mentioned you were  talking about a mentor  um ricardo you mentioned a mentor in one  of your other classes  do these kind of relationships come up  organically are they mostly  um things that come through structured  formats and classrooms how have you seen  um mentorship advisor sponsor  relationships kind of develop across  campus  yeah for me it was pretty organic my  professor reached out and said hey i  wrote a book about founders of  nonprofits do you want to connect with  this one and i said yes of course i do  and  we had a call we hit it off and then he  sent me a really kind email afterwards  and so  i was like okay this person seems  incredible his name is reid soros and  i wanted to to learn from him since his  nonprofit has done great work in the  education opportunity space and so i  just asked him hey do you want to be my  mentor for the summer while i'm  exploring this idea  and he agreed and then we just continue  working together from there  i think other people might do some code  emails to find mentors in a more  a more formal sense but for me it was  just organically talking to people and  then see who you connect with and going  from there  yeah i i um so there is there are formal  processes for it in  um that i went through and uh started  garage and hacking for defense where you  get matched up with a mentor  though those are are also hit or miss  just like in in normal life if you go  out and try to find a mentor it can work  sometimes and sometimes it doesn't  um we were fortunate that one of the one  of the  folks that was one of our mentors was  amazing and we hit it off and  you know the the sessions were great but  we you know there were examples when  that wasn't quite the case and then  so there are a lot of um you know  examples  uh that are more structured but there  are you know to shawan's point  um i i actually did probably the person  who's helped me the most is somebody  that  i reached out to via cold email um you  know that per  you know i talked to a whole bunch of  people but that person was the one that  i connected with the most and i just  kept  you know having more questions for and  kept connecting with  and they agreed to and they provided all  sorts of guidance and  things like that and i think it's it can  happen very organically as well just  as part of you know if you're willing to  be coached you'll find someone  jacqueline here's a question for you how  did you think about the cost of the mba  so we're taking a  whole left turn here um kind of coming  back to the beginning of the journey  but how did you think about the cost of  the mba  including opportunity cost of leaving uh  lucrative career you know finance  in the context of wanting to start a  company like how did you kind of  approach that mentally yeah um  i'm not doing a startup because i think  it's gonna make me a ton of money i mean  that would be great as a side effect but  uh the  reason i'm doing it is i think it's the  best conduit for personal and  professional growth  um you know the actual content you know  i deal with irs rules on taxability  benefits when i was five years old i did  not dream that's not like going to space  you know that was not my dream  um but the lessons learned along the way  are like why i wanted to do it to start  with and that's  what sustains me when things get really  tough  and you know your first five or ten  customer calls and they tell you  your idea is not great or they would  never buy it or whatever it is like i  think that's that's what sustains me  through it  um so i didn't think of starting a  company  as like a in the in the rational way i  would say that you're presenting it  that way it was never for me about that  um i i think even if i had taken the  money i invested in my mba  and said i'm going to put in a bank  account and start spending that money  i don't think i would have had a clue on  how to actually do that in a good way i  mean  like i said i didn't have an idea i  didn't have a process i didn't have a  co-founder  and so those are other very important  ingredients other than money  for really to start something of your  own um  so i guess i yeah my answer is like  i didn't think about it in that way and  um you know while i  if you have a fully fleshed out idea and  all you really need is capital to make  it work yeah i mean i i think  you can make the case for that but for  me it was about getting the other  ingredients  um for starting a company  and here's a question from michael um  thinking about  opportunities to continue working or  launching something outside of the  classroom during the academic year  is that possible how do you juggle your  time  were you continuing to work on your  ideas  outside of the classroom like after  those classes have been over  yeah definitely go ahead yeah sorry yeah  certainly um i think it's it's it's kind  of you know you  you manage your own time the the way  that you want to and you kind of  prioritize things as as you see fit  right i think you you you  are advised not to work at  a startup while you're in um while  you're doing your  uh your mba but there are people that do  it right so you people have done it and  like  they have great experiences and and kind  of learned a lot and have a lot of  personal growth  and then there's tons of people that are  working on ideas whether they're doing  it through coursework or not and they  spend a lot of time  outside of school doing that and being  around your  classmates helps um quite a bit in terms  of like  you know breaking down barriers that  that you're facing  and and all that as well  yeah that hits on a lot of what i was  going to bring up it it's hard  to to work on your idea and also be a  student and have a social life  um but i think if you come in and you  say this is what i want to do and i know  i'm committed  to at least exploring the idea you block  the time on your calendar before  somebody else sends you an  invite for for an event and for me it  was worth it i  i said no to a lot of stuff at gsb so i  could be off campus going to schools and  pilot  my idea but i'm in the long run in line  with what i wanted to do out after gsb  and i still had time to like make good  friends and have fun and and all of that  so it's not like you you sacrifice  everything you just have to be a bit  more intentional  the other thing is that there are a lot  of classes where you can work on your  venture and do things for the class so  basically any class that i was in i  found a way to tie to vocal justice so  i was in winning writing that was a  communications course i would have my  pitches be about vocal justice  or my non-profit management class i  would do research on a competitor that i  needed to research anyway for vocal  justice  and so it ends up being less work in the  end  since you have a dual purpose for it and  then schwann did you do  a low key note as well yes i did a low  keynote which is a nine minute talk on  the issue you care about and mine was  related to vocal justice it was  on public speaking and critical  consciousness and i use that now to  promote the organization  these are also accessible so if you go  to youtube and search low key notes you  can also see what that looks like so  encourage folks to take to check that  out as well  then jaclyn did you pop off did you have  a nope  nothing awesome and so we're talking  about like  intentionality with time i think that  can also be an intentionality with  opportunities  um and the question from jay is like how  did you  balance you know driving towards that  idea versus  staying open and exploring like other  things like any advice to someone who's  grappling  with those same that same tension  i think by being around classmates who  are doing  lots of different things it comes you're  by now  like by default kind of open just from  you know dinner  like lunchtime dinner time conversation  what's like what's everyone interested  in what's everyone focused on and so  you'll just learn by default a lot of  what other pursuits could look like um  so i guess for me uh i  i felt like it was very clear like what  the different flavors of and if anything  i could draw inspiration from my  classmates of like how they were  pursuing it in their way  um that gave me ideas on like you know  how i might inform my own process  nice one thing i would just add there  quickly is  that um as long as you're committed to  sort of either being an entrepreneur or  joining a startup i think  things become way easier because if  you're also trying to recruit for like  um consulting and pe then things become  more difficult because you're basically  recruiting the entire  year but if you commit to kind of being  either an entrepreneur or going to work  at a startup  then you have a ton of time to explore  right you don't have to commit to  something immediately or if you do  commit to something  for a month and you decide to pivot at  the end of that  then it's not a waste of time because  you still have more months to go right  so i  i wouldn't be too concerned about that  it's more just making the decision  between  am i going to go the traditional kind of  consulting pe route or am i going to go  the  entrepreneurship startup route  and we're getting close to time and so  i'm going to go ahead and claim this  last  question as you're thinking you know  words of wisdom to a younger you whether  it's in the application process it's  during your time at the gsb  maybe it's just as you graduated as  you're  venturing out onto this whole new whole  new brave world ahead  like what advice would you give to  yourself  um so take a moment and then maybe  ricardo we could start with you and john  then jacqueline  to take us home  yeah i would i would say i mean first  and foremost like breathe  right it's like a it's it's a it's a bit  of a long process and it's  super fun it's really transformative  from a personal  and uh you know educational perspective  um you're gonna meet a ton of  amazing people who you never thought you  would be kind of rubbing  rubbing elbows with and stuff but um  just breathe for now and  just know that you know the the world's  going to be your oyster once you're here  you're going to have tons of  opportunities to meet people  um have conversations you never thought  you'd have and you know  learn topics that you never thought  you'd kind of be diving into  thank you show on i would say  take time for yourself and i think there  are two ways to think about that one is  just time for you to breathe relax binge  watch that show on netflix because the  entrepreneurship journey  is hectic and so you got to just take  that time and be diligent about taking  care of yourself but  the the other way i think about that is  time for introspection  i think a lot of my best thinking comes  when i'm just sitting by myself for a  couple of hours and  free writing about why i'm doing the  work that i'm doing what my values are  what some of my concerns are and how i  want to get better  and it's really hard to make that time  on your own if you're just go go go with  your  your venture and so being diligent about  having that time maybe every two weeks  every month  um to have some introspection i think is  important  awesome and then jaclyn taking us home  uh well the first uh ricardo and  chawan's answers were both really good  uh  you know it's all gonna be fine um i  would say  uh have a plan and be patient  um so i think for me you know having a  few  areas of focus um help me make  really easy not easy but like easier  decisions given just the  wealth of things you could be doing at  the gsb and  you know your calendar will be booked  end to end and probably double book  because there's so many cool things that  you want to be doing  um and so you need some level of focus  of like what  you know what you want your experience  to be oriented around  you know a small a few things um and and  really spend time on those two things  um and then finally i would say you know  i think one  sense of feeling i had was like oh i see  so and so doing this and that's really  taking off  i see so-and-so doing this and that's  really taking off and  i think in hindsight um you know having  a bit more patience with myself that  like you know for me my entrepreneurship  journey bloomed kind of post business  school and  i wasn't one of the ones that kind of  came in um with a clear vision in mind  um but it still kind of you know worked  out the way that it was  meant to work out so be patient and have  a plan  wonderful so breathe take time for  yourself  be patient have a plan these are all  really really wise words to live by  definitely stuff that i'm gonna be  applying to my own life even though i'm  not in  entrepreneurship at the moment but with  that thank you  so much to our panelists who took your  time out today to  to spend with us for those of you in the  audience if you haven't done so already  i would  definitely encourage you to jump onto  mystanford.mba  you can create a dashboard there and  with a few questions  we'll be able to help collect ideas  events that might be of interest to you  speaking of events beginning of august  august 2nd  through 6th we are hosting an  application week to help you better  understand the process  and so in doing that to help you put  your best foot forward  uh there was some a lot of themes about  process  and hopefully that is one place that  we're going to be able to dig into that  even more deeply  with that thank you again for joining us  and we hope to see you across  other events this summer stay safe um  hope to see  you

